; systemd timer for the certificate-expiry collector.
;
; DAILY, unlike the container-metrics timer's 30 seconds. A certificate's notAfter does not move,
; so the only thing a shorter period would buy is noticing a REPLACED file sooner -- and the alert
; thresholds are measured in days and weeks. Once a day is two orders of magnitude finer than the
; decision it feeds.
;
; 03:40, inside the 04:00 maintenance window's approach but before the backup at 04:15, so a day's
; metrics are fresh before the nightly work starts. RandomizedDelaySec spreads it because nothing
; downstream cares about the exact minute.

[Unit]
Description=Profit Basetool — certificate expiry metrics, daily
Documentation=https://github.com/krt-profit/basetool/blob/main/scripts/cert-expiry-metrics.py
; No Requires= on the service: a timer only TRIGGERS it, through Unit= below. A Requires= also
; starts the service whenever the timer itself starts -- at every boot, when timers.target pulls the
; timer in -- without the timer elapsing (docs/arc42/07-deployment-view.md §7.4b). Guarded by
; scripts/container-runtime.test.sh.

[Timer]
; Once shortly after boot as well: a freshly provisioned or rebooted host should not wait until the
; next 03:40 to report what its certificates say.
OnBootSec=5min
OnCalendar=*-*-* 03:40:00
RandomizedDelaySec=300

; Catch up a missed run. Unlike the container metrics -- where a late write describes a moment that
; has passed -- an expiry date is equally true whenever it is read, so a catch-up is strictly better
; than a gap.
Persistent=true

Unit=iri-cert-expiry.service

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
